build the cart faces two past the screen, so scrolling never waits on one

The shelf stalled for a frame and a half now and then while scrolling.
CartFaces::sync blocks the frame on every on-screen face it does not hold,
and since 0016 the only faces asked for were the drawn span itself -- so
every step along the shelf brought a cart on screen whose face had not been
started yet, and the frame waited for a worker to decode its label and
build it. 0011's instrument caught it on the handheld: nine waits past 2 ms
over 19,200 frames of scrolling, the worst 26.0 and 24.3 ms, each one a
dropped frame or two on a shelf that otherwise holds 60 Hz.

Now the current shelf asks for, and keeps, two carts past each end of the
drawn span as well. They are not marked on screen, so nothing waits for
them; by the time a step brings one into view it has been built behind the
scroll. Two rather than one because holding a direction repeats every 50 ms
and there are two workers, so each has two steps' time to build a face.
That is four more faces on the shelf, 0.84 MB at 312x337, against the 3.4 MB
0016 took back; while a game plays the rule is unchanged, the seated cart
only, so none of it is held behind a running core.

Tested on the host (rust:1.96-bookworm): a new test steps the cursor right
and left and asserts that every cart on screen after a step was already
asked for before it. It fails with FACE_AHEAD at 0 and passes at 2. 0016's
span tests are updated to the new numbers. The rest of slot and slot-ui's
tests fail in the same 25 places with and without this patch.

Traced on the Flip (2026-10-31): scrolling along a shelf no longer waits
for a face. Moving to another shelf still does, 36-100 ms once, while its
faces are built for the first time -- the cost 0016 chose to keep the
memory, and left alone here.

Applies on top of 0035, upstream 91499a9.

